  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\n- Breezy Saturday with a small chance of showers\n\n Not a whole lot of change in the short range pattern. A mid level\ntrough emanating from an upper level low will swing through the\nregion tomorrow. This will bring stronger flow through northern\nlower Michigan Saturday afternoon. A stronger gradient will set\nup. That gradient will bring gusty winds, 20 to 30 mph, to the \nregion with the strongest winds expected along and north of the I \n96 corridor. The best moisture will be north of the US 10 corridor\nwith only a small chance of showers southward. \n\n- Showers and Storms Possible Tuesday through midweek\n\nA significant upper level system will eject out of the northern\nrockies and swing through Canada early next week. Latest model \ntrends show the cold front associated with this system stalls. \nFrontal passage through the Great Lakes should be closer to \nTuesday. \n\nThis system will bring best chance for precipitation with\nshowers and storm possible Tuesday. The frontal pattern continues\nto show the potential to stall with the boundary draping across \nsouthern Michigan through IN and IL. That stalled frontal\nboundary, its associated moisture and the fact that there could be\ndecent warm air advection could bring a prolonged period of \nshower/storm activity midweek into Thursday.",
